<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#silver">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Silver</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Silver is a precious metal used throughout history for coinage, jewellery and as a store of value. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> it was a standard of value, and it was central to the coinage of the Punic, Seleucid and Carolingian empires. Spanish silver from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> transformed the economies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> and Ming and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a>, while silver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> flowed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silver.jpg" alt="Silver" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alchemist-hp · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_crystal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
